ere's a certain logic to talking to  the spd and the fdp now that doesn't  mean that we rule out talking to the  conservatives  the other would-be king makers the  business-friendly fdp even went as fast  to suggest that they would speak to the  greens before approaching either of the  two main parties  whatever the outcome of the coalition  talks it looks like the new government  in berlin will be made up of three  parties  an arrangement that germany hasn't seen  in more than half a century  after the last general election four  years ago negotiations dragged on for  over four months  schultz promised that with him in charge  there would be a speedier resolution  my idea is  that we will be very fast in getting a  result for this government and it should  be before christmas if possible  until then angela merkel will continue  to hold the fort her time in office  extends until a new chancellor is ready  to take over  so the coalition building starts now  let's take a look at how that process  normally works the party with the  highest vote share in this case the espd  usually takes a first shot at putting  together a government they'll hold talks  with their potential coalition partners  the greens and the fdp and if there's  enough common ground they'll draw up a  coalition agreement  the haggling can take months after the  last election germany had to wait  171 days for a new government if  coalition talks between all the parties  fail there are two options you can form  a minority government or hold fresh  elections  chancellor mackel will stay in office as  a caretaker in a caretaker capacity  until a new government is formed  well for the very latest let's bring in  dw correspondent julia saudelli uh julia  spd leader olaf scholz he says he is  going to form a new government that he  has a mandate for that and that he hopes  he can get a coalition together by  christmas but how certain is it that the  social democrats will actually lead the  next government  well in politics as in life nothing is  certain but it looks like at the moment  the social democrats are let's say in  the lead in terms of looking of  looking at forming a new a new  government and that is something that  also resonates with german voters some  polls have shown that a majority of  germans want a three-way coalition  between the spd the greens and the free  democrats fdp and it looks like this is  what's going to happen we have  heard here  members of the spd saying they're ready  to start going into talks already this  week if the other two parties say  they're ready so they're looking to get  started on these talks but anything can  happen we've seen it happen before  coalition talks collapsing so even  though it looks like the spd will have a  first dibs at trying to start a  coalition it doesn't necessarily mean  that they'll be able to form one  successfully  okay so while the social democrats are  trying to do that you've got the cdu on  the other hand chancellor mackel's party  in this case led by armen lashed he's  even though  they did not win the election this time  he says he will still try to form a  government too how realistic is that  well a lot of that depends on how much  amin lashed manages to um maintain his  uh his standing in the party we've heard  uh some disgruntled comments within uh  the parties some members calling for  lashed to resign others saying that they  really don't see a mandate to form a  government and that the ceu should do  some soul searching before they decide  to take lead again  but amin lashit seems determined to hold  on to the hope and possibility and he  said he he is available and as we've  said um the coalition talks between the  other parties could collapse and then  the cdu could eventually uh play a role  in terms of possibly forming a  government if these talks between the  spd the greens and the fdp do fail  okay but the first step we've got the  social democrats talking to the probably  the greek the greens and the fdp trying  to put together a three-way coalition  what are the main challenges julia to  forming that coalition  well between the social democrats and  the greens there are some closer points  of contact in terms of party programs  the two parties had said before  the election and had said that again  that they were happy to uh possibly  govern together and now the uh the joker  fdp has come into play and um  this complicates the situation a little  bit because in terms of  red lines for example the free democrats  have said that one of their red lines as  is that they don't want to see tax  increases while the other two parties  had put forward the idea of maybe  increasing taxes for the richer part of  the population for example for the  greens to finance ambitious climate  goals  also in terms of climate goals the fdp  has slightly a different approach  especially compared to the greens so  there are some points there that are  going to have to be clarified it's going  to be all about compromise and  finding  common points that the three parties can  agree on  julia thank you so much our political  correspondent julia saudelli there at  the german rights time  well as we just heard it looks  increasingly likely that one of the  biggest obstacles to forging a new  governing coalition here in germany will  be squaring the parties different  positions on tackling climate change the  free democrats and the greens both agree  it's an urgent problem but they have  very different ideas about how to fix it  tens of thousands on the streets of  berlin  aiming to put climate front and center  of the next german government  with a helping hand by an activist  superstar  we want change  we demand change  and we are the change  what exactly change means depends on  which party you speak to  for the greens devastating floods in the  summer were evidence of the crisis they  had long warned about  to fix it they want a ban on new  combustion car engines speed limits  massive rail investment and to speed up  the exit from coal  we finally need a government that will  fight for the climate with all its might  with all its heart and with passion  for the free democrats you could  substitute climate with business  the only things they want to ban are tax  rises and spending splurges  they're counting on innovation and the  market to solve the problem  [Applause]  our responsibility is to produce the  technologies that make it happen  at home but also in the world  an economic perspective a free way of  life reconciled with climate protection  little separates the two biggest parties  all of schultz's social democrats and  armen lashed christian democrats promise  to hit net zero carbon dioxide emissions  by 2045  and exit coal in 2038  for them the message is that the scale  of the upheaval requires careful  management  [Music]  we will only be able to tackle the big  tasks such as the energy transition or  the climate turnaround with growth and  new economic dynamics which will require  a lot of resources  it's the biggest industrial and economic  modernisation project that germany has  had to undertake in well over 100 years  we want to tackle this and we can do it  if we start now  again as the horse trading begins in  berlin  the question for the parties is where  are they willing to budge  well for more let's speak with lisa  bardum she's the climate policy  spokesperson for the greens in the  german parliament thanks for being with  us ms bottom if your party does enter  government it will be as a junior  partner in a three-way coalition what  makes you think you can push through  your climate agenda in those  negotiations with other parties who have  different ideas about how to address the  climate crisis  good morning first of all  what we've seen with the election is a  clear mandate for attacking the climate  crisis we gained nearly six percent and  our main topic  was  climate protection and especially the  young younger generation they voted for  us  so people below 30 um they had greens at  the first place  so i think um it's quite clear that this  topic gains percentages and um  yeah people want something to go forward  whereas the conservatives  they lost this election so  for me the direction is quite clear  okay but you really haven't addressed  how you're going to overcome the  differences with the other parties in  coalition negotiations just a few days  ago you were tweeting about  how inadequate other parties in germany  are on climate policy you posted a  picture of olaf schultz whose spd is  first in line to lead the next  government you accused him of climate  failures as a policy maker  is your party willing and indeed able to  work under a leader you describe as a  failure in addressing your top priority  that's true we had a failure in climate  politics  in the last years of the great the grand  coalition i mean that's why i wanted to  have a change that's why we did a  campaign for the election and that's why  the greens are so important in  government  and every party i mean except the fd  addressed the topic of climate  protection so every party has to deliver  now if it's sbd or ftp or  wherever party  and i think uh i think we're open for  any proposals how to reduce co2 but we  have to have proposal and it's not  enough to have some rhetoric so um  we're ready to talk with the other  parties and  but we can't do it in public of course  we can do it in in the media  what are your  non-negotiables moving into this if your  party well here link put it another way  is your party prepared to walk out of  coalition talks if you feel you're not  getting your agenda across  i think it's quite clear  that we have to phase out coal earlier  and that we have to increase the  percentage of renewable energies because  um yeah we cannot  get we cannot reach our goals without  that and i think the red line should be  the german climate targets we all  um agree to them in in the bundestag so  we should adjust to that  lisa bottom member of the german  parliament from the green party thank  you very much  thank you  well for the conservative cdu csu block  this election delivered the worst ever  national result several prominent  christian democrats lost in their  constituencies without angela meckel  leading the charge the party's most  important driving force was missing  something that was very clear in  mackel's own constituency  parliamentary constituency 15 covers a  picturesque corner of germany the island  of rugen and the city of greifsbelt on  the baltic coast  this was merkel country angela merkel  held the seat as the directly elected  member of the bundestag at every  election since 1990 but sunday's vote  changed all that to replace the cdu  chancellor the voters here have chosen a  left-wing social democrat  everything that has been around for a  while wears out at some point and if the  things you want to change don't change  eventually you try  i think that things will be fine with  the spd and after all they will  certainly not govern alone that's why i  believe the fact that the cdu lost here  is definitely very good for young people  the whole conservative campaign didn't  speak to me  schultz was much more effective  merkel was in the constituency just a  week ago at a rain-soaked campaign event  with cdu leader amin lashette the  chancellor was hoping her popularity  would boost georg gunter the christian  democrat aiming to succeed her in this  seat but what if she had decided to  stand herself  she can't believe hers first  i can well imagine that she would have  won more votes than the cdu did in fact  get there might have been a kind of  strategic approach by voters to elect a  powerful woman in the hope that our area  will be represented accordingly but on  the other hand there's a clear move  we've had enough  now we want something different  the lack of enthusiasm for the  conservatives here is not an isolated  case across a huge swathe of germany  many formerly cdu seats were flipped by  the spd  as well as a massive drop in their share  of the overall vote these losses are a  further sign merkel's party needs to  rethink its appeal  now that the chancellor herself is  departing from the scene  you

